      "body": "## Purpose\n\nMoE routing kernels consume `topk_ids` and `topk_weights` as dense linear\nbuffers. A sliced routing result such as `wide[:, :top_k]` can have shape\n`(num_tokens, top_k)` but stride `(wide_top_k, 1)`. The previous\n`topk_weights.stride(1) == 1` checks accepted this layout, while the kernels\nignored the row stride and could silently read incorrect routing data.\n\nThis change fails fast without introducing hidden copies:\n\n- validate `topk_ids` in the `moe_align_block_size` C++ wrapper before the\n  CUDA kernel launch;\n- require fully contiguous `topk_weights` at the shared MoE dispatch boundary;\n- apply the same validation in the direct Triton launcher used by the modular\n  Triton backend.\n\n## Test Plan\n\n- Add a regression test passing sliced, non-contiguous `topk_ids` to\n  `moe_align_block_size`.\n- Add a regression test passing sliced, non-contiguous `topk_weights` to the\n  Triton MoE launcher.\n- Run the relevant formatting and static-analysis hooks.\n\n## Test Result\n\nPassed:\n\n- `ruff-check`\n- `ruff-format`\n- `clang-format`\n- `typos`\n- `check-spdx-header`\n- `git diff --check`\n- Python bytecode compilation for the changed Python files\n\nThe CUDA regression test was not run locally because the source-tree C++\nextension was not rebuilt. The repository virtual environment also cannot run\npytest currently because it lacks `tblib` and contains an incompatible PyTorch\ninstallation; CI is expected to run both regression tests against a matching\nbuild.\n\n## AI assistance\n\nOpenAI Codex was used for investigation, implementation, and test\norchestration. The submitter reviewed all changed lines before submission.\n",
